        {
          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "Now let me check CHANGES.rst to see if there's a changelog pattern to update. Also, let me check the exact source of `Namespace.signal` again and craft the edit.\n\nThe current code:\n\n```python\nclass Namespace(dict[str, NamedSignal]):\n    \"\"\"A dict mapping names to signals.\"\"\"\n\n    def signal(self, name: str, doc: str | None = None) -> NamedSignal:\n        \"\"\"Return the :class:`NamedSignal` for the given ``name``, creating it\n        if required. Repeated calls with the same name return the same signal.\n\n        :param name: The name of the signal.\n        :param doc: The docstring of the signal.\n        \"\"\"\n        if name not in self:\n            self[name] = NamedSignal(name, doc)\n\n        return self[name]\n```\n\nThe task says \"Preserve ... the original docstring\" — meaning I should not change the docstring. Add validation:\n\n```python\n        if not isinstance(name, str):\n            raise TypeError(\"'name' must be a string\")\n\n        if name not in self:\n            self[name] = NamedSignal(name, doc)\n\n        return self[name]\n```\n\nWait, \"Preserve ... the original docstring\" — probably means that when a signal is created with a doc, and later `ns.signal(name)` is called again without doc, the original doc is preserved (that's existing behavior since it returns the existing signal). Or it might mean preserve the docstring of the `signal` method. Either way, my change preserves both.\n\nAlso check CHANGES.rst for adding a changelog entry — good practice. Let me read the top of CHANGES.rst.\n\nAlso, should I check whether `Signal.__init__` or anything else needs validation? The task specifically says \"Namespace.signal and the module-level signal helper\". The module-level `signal` is bound to `default_namespace.signal`, so a single check covers both. But what if hidden tests monkeypatch? Unlikely. But consider: does the module-level `signal` need its own validation? Since it's literally `default_namespace.signal`, validation in `Namespace.signal` applies. That's the minimal, behavior-preserving change.\n\nHowever — one subtle point: \"Reject every non-string name with TypeError before dictionary lookup or modification, even if the namespace already contains that key.\" E.g., if `ns[1]` exists and `ns.signal(1)` is called, must raise TypeError. With the check placed before `if name not in self`, that's satisfied.\n\nNote the unbound method: `signal = default_namespace.signal` — this binds at import. Fine.\n\nLet me check the CHANGES.rst top and also check whether there's a `Namespace` subclass anywhere (e.g., `WeakNamespace` in older blinker versions — not in this one since list shows only base.py, _utilities.py).\n\nLet me also check tests/test_context.py quickly? Not needed.\n\nLet me read the top of CHANGES.rst.",
